@page "/movies/{Key}"
@using System.Globalization
@implements IDisposable
@inject CatalogService Catalog
@inject Announcer Announcer
@inject IJSRuntime Js

@* One film, everything the catalogue holds about it.

   THE PAGE TITLE AND THE h1 ARE IN THE FIRST RENDER AND OUTSIDE EVERY STATE BRANCH, for the reason
   Movies.razor records: App.razor's FocusOnNavigate fires once after the first render and never retries, and
   the browser gate's own boot signal is "#app main#main-content h1". A heading that appeared only once the
   film arrived would cost the focus move AND every A11y test its wait.

   No live region of any kind in here: no alert role, no status role, no log role, and no live-region
   attribute. (Spelled in words, because SourceContractTests matches these files for the literal markers and a
   comment quoting one fails exactly as the attribute would.) The announcer in MainLayout owns every region. *@
<PageTitle>@Heading - Film and TV library</PageTitle>

@* tabindex="-1" for ElementReference.FocusAsync(), which -- unlike FocusOnNavigate's helper -- does not add
   the attribute and fails silently without it. Not a conformance claim; a feature dependency. *@
<h1 tabindex="-1" @ref="heading">@Heading</h1>

@* ONE STABLE CONTAINER IN EVERY STATE, never sibling branches that swap nodes: replacing the node would
   destroy the one a screen reader's virtual cursor may be sitting on while the film loads.

   The paragraph has something true to say in all four states, which is why it can stay. Three of them are a
   sentence; the fourth is the film's own byline -- year, runtime, rating -- which is what a detail page puts
   under its title anyway. So this is not an empty element kept alive for the sake of a rule.

   .film-state AND NOT .catalog-state. The browser gate keys its settle wait off the films page's container
   ("if .catalog-state is present, wait for the tile grid"), so reusing that class would have every scan of
   this page wait for a grid that never comes. RouteScanTests.SettledAsync knows both names. *@
<div class="film-state" tabindex="-1" @ref="stateContainer">
    @if (film is { } byline)
    {
        @* The same two strings the tile renders, from the same helper, and the gap between them is a flex
           gap with NO separator character in the DOM -- a middot is read inconsistently and ::before
           content is exposed to the accessibility tree all the same. The rating is guarded exactly as the
           tile's is: Vote is 0d on every unmatched record, and "0.0/10" would state as a fact that a film
           nobody looked up scored zero. *@
        <p class="film-byline">
            @if (byline.Y is { } year)
            {
                <span>@year</span>
            }
            <span>@FilmText.Runtime(byline.Rt)</span>
            @if (byline.Vote > 0d)
            {
                <span>@FilmText.Rating(byline.Vote)</span>
            }
        </p>
    }
    else
    {
        <p>@Sentence</p>
    }

    @if (films.State == CatalogState.Failed || retrying)
    {
        @* aria-disabled, never the disabled ATTRIBUTE: disabling the focused element blurs it and drops
           focus to <body>. The click still arrives and is ignored in TryAgain instead. *@
        <button type="button" class="try-again" aria-disabled="@(retrying ? "true" : null)" @onclick="TryAgain">Try again</button>
    }
</div>

@if (film is { } f)
{
    @* THE ARTWORK, AND BOTH IMAGES ARE DECORATIVE. alt="" on each: the title is the h1 immediately above,
       so a description here would be the film named twice, and a backdrop is set dressing with no
       information in it at all. A MISSING alt would be the defect and is indistinguishable in a browser,
       which is why MarkupContractTests requires the attribute rather than preferring it.

       NO <img> AT ALL WHEN THERE IS NO PATH, and the test is `is { Length: > 0 }` rather than a null check:
       a published record can carry p: "" , and an empty src renders broken-image chrome in some browsers
       and is exposed as text by others. The poster falls back to the same placeholder box the tile uses; an
       absent backdrop renders nothing, because a placeholder for set dressing is set dressing for set
       dressing.

       width and height are the intrinsic sizes of the w1280 and w342 renditions. They reserve the box
       before the TMDB CDN answers, which matters most under magnification. The stylesheet returns the
       height to auto for the reason PosterTile.razor.css records at length: an HTML height attribute is a
       presentational hint that pins the used height, and aspect-ratio only resolves a dimension that is
       auto, so without it the artwork is stretched rather than scaled.

       NO TEXT OVER EITHER IMAGE. Text on artwork has no contrast ratio that can be measured or promised --
       every film's backdrop is a different picture -- so SC 1.4.3 cannot be satisfied by inspection. The
       title, the byline and the tagline are all in the flow above and beside it. *@
    @if (f.B is { Length: > 0 } backdrop)
    {
        <img class="film-backdrop" src="@($"https://image.tmdb.org/t/p/w1280{backdrop}")" alt=""
             width="1280" height="720" decoding="async" />
    }

    <div class="film-art">
        @if (f.P is { Length: > 0 } poster)
        {
            <img class="film-poster" src="@($"https://image.tmdb.org/t/p/w342{poster}")" alt=""
                 width="342" height="513" decoding="async" />
        }
        else
        {
            <span class="film-poster-placeholder" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        }

        <div class="film-blurb">
            @* THE TAGLINE IS NOT A HEADING AND NOT AN ITALIC DECORATION. It is a sentence the studio wrote,
               so it is a paragraph; <em> would claim a stress emphasis the words do not have, and a heading
               would put marketing copy into the outline a reader navigates by. *@
            @if (f.Tag is { Length: > 0 } tagline)
            {
                <p class="film-tagline">@tagline</p>
            }

            @* PLAIN div AND h2, NEVER <section>. A section with an accessible name becomes a region
               landmark, and four of them on one page would file Overview, Cast, Details and Additional
               information in a landmark list beside Banner, Navigation and Main -- which is what a landmark
               list is for, and these are not that. Unnamed, a section maps to generic and buys nothing at
               all. The h2 is what a reader navigates by. *@
            @if (f.Ov is { Length: > 0 } overview)
            {
                <div class="film-section">
                    <h2>Overview</h2>
                    <p>@overview</p>
                </div>
            }
        </div>
    </div>
    @* THE CAST, AS A LIST, BECAUSE IT IS ONE. role="list" is declared by hand for the reason PosterGrid
       records: a list whose markers CSS removes is stripped of its list semantics by Safari and VoiceOver,
       and the role puts them back. Every photo is alt="" -- the person's name is the text beside it.

       "as" IS A REAL TEXT NODE AND NOT A SEPARATOR. "Brad Pitt as Mills" reads as a sentence; "Brad Pitt --
       Mills" reads as "Brad Pitt" then, depending on the reader's punctuation level, either nothing or
       "dash". No truncation either, in C# or in CSS: a character name cut short cannot be recovered, and
       there is no link, label or title attribute here that could carry the rest.

       A character this record has no name for renders the person alone rather than "Brad Pitt as". The
       pipeline writes string.Empty for a missing character, so the test is on Length and not on null. *@
    @if (f.Cast is { Length: > 0 } cast)
    {
        <div class="film-section">
            <h2>Cast</h2>

            <ul class="film-cast" role="list">
                @foreach (var member in cast)
                {
                    <li>
                        @if (member.P is { Length: > 0 } portrait)
                        {
                            <img src="@($"https://image.tmdb.org/t/p/w185{portrait}")" alt=""
                                 width="185" height="278" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
                        }
                        else
                        {
                            <span class="film-portrait-placeholder" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                        }

                        <span class="film-cast-name">@member.N</span>
                        @if (member.C is { Length: > 0 } character)
                        {
                            <span class="film-cast-role">as @character</span>
                        }
                    </li>
                }
            </ul>
        </div>
    }

    @* THE FACTS, AS A DESCRIPTION LIST, WITH EACH PAIR WRAPPED IN A div. The wrapper is valid HTML (the
       spec allows one div per group inside a dl) and is what lets the pair be a grid cell without
       display: contents, which Chrome and Safari both used to drop from the accessibility tree.

       EVERY ROW IS GUARDED, and none of them is padded with a placeholder. "Unknown" in a dd is the page
       stating that the library knows the answer is unknown, when what is true is that the catalogue has no
       field for it; an absent row says exactly that and says it shorter.

       THE MATCH QUALITY IS TEXT HERE AND NOT THE TILE'S BADGE, deliberately. The badge exists because a
       wall of sixty posters has no room for a label, and its rules are scoped to PosterTile.razor.css --
       a second copy of them here is the drift this project keeps extracting helpers to avoid. On a page
       with room for a description list, the band's own words in a dd beside the score say more than a
       bordered box does, and they come from ConfidenceBand so the page, the tile and the build summary
       cannot disagree. *@
    <div class="film-section">
        <h2>Details</h2>

        <dl class="film-facts">
            @if (f.Dir is { Length: > 0 })
            {
                <div>
                    <dt>@(f.Dir.Length == 1 ? "Director" : "Directors")</dt>
                    <dd>@string.Join(", ", f.Dir)</dd>
                </div>
            }

            @if (GenreNames(f) is { Length: > 0 } genres)
            {
                <div>
                    <dt>Genres</dt>
                    <dd>@genres</dd>
                </div>
            }

            @if (f.Ag is { Length: > 0 } appleGenre)
            {
                <div>
                    <dt>Category in the library</dt>
                    <dd>@appleGenre</dd>
                </div>
            }

            @if (f.Col is { Length: > 0 } collection)
            {
                <div>
                    <dt>Collection</dt>
                    <dd>@collection</dd>
                </div>
            }

            @* "Bought as part of" AND NOT "How it was bought", which this said first. MovieRecord.Own is
               the Apple library's bundle column, and in the live catalogue every one of its 721 distinct
               values is the name of a set -- "Paramount Pictures 50-Movie Collection", "The James Bond
               Collection" -- and never a price, a date or a shop. A label that promises how and answers
               with what reads as a field that went wrong. *@
            @if (f.Own is { Length: > 0 } purchase)
            {
                <div>
                    <dt>Bought as part of</dt>
                    <dd>@purchase</dd>
                </div>
            }

            <div>
                <dt>Match</dt>
                <dd>@MatchSentence(f)</dd>
            </div>
        </dl>
    </div>

    @* THE OWNER'S OWN FIELDS. MovieRecord.Ex is a contract: a key added by hand to the record store
       survives every build in between with no code change, and nothing in this project interprets the
       contents. So this renders whatever is there and reasons about none of it.

       ORDERED BY StringComparer.Ordinal AND NOT BY THE DICTIONARY'S ORDER. Dictionary<,> enumerates in an
       order no documentation promises, and a page whose rows moved between two loads of the same film would
       be SC 3.2.3 Consistent Navigation's problem in miniature -- a reader who learned where a row was
       would find it somewhere else. Ordinal and not culture-aware, so the order cannot depend on the
       browser's locale.

       A BLANK KEY OR A BLANK VALUE IS SKIPPED. A dt with no text is a term a reader is told exists and
       cannot read, and a dd with no text is the same from the other side; both are the shape that passes
       every automated check and says nothing. @key is on the wrapper so Blazor diffs these rows by their
       own identity rather than by position: without it, removing one row re-labels every row after it for
       one frame. *@
    @if (ExtraFields(f) is { Count: > 0 } extra)
    {
        <div class="film-section">
            <h2>Additional information</h2>

            <dl class="film-facts">
                @foreach (var (name, value) in extra)
                {
                    <div @key="name">
                        <dt>@name</dt>
                        <dd>@value</dd>
                    </div>
                }
            </dl>
        </div>
    }
}

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The failure sentence. ONE constant for the visible paragraph and for the <c>Alert</c>, so the seen
    /// and the spoken copies cannot drift. Internal so a test can assert they are the same string rather
    /// than two literals that happen to match today.
    /// </summary>
    internal const string CouldNotLoad =
        "This film could not be loaded. Check your connection and try again.";

    /// <summary>
    /// What a key with no film behind it says, and it says the ADDRESS is wrong rather than the library.
    /// A deep link to a film the owner has since removed, or a key mistyped in a bookmark, is not a fetch
    /// that failed, so it offers no Try again: no number of retries will resolve it.
    /// </summary>
    internal const string NoSuchFilm = "No film in the library has this address.";

    /// <summary>The visible loading copy, with the ellipsis CHARACTER rather than three full stops.</summary>
    internal const string LoadingVisible = "Loading film…";

    /// <summary>
    /// The heading in each state, and each one is a claim the page can stand behind. "Film not found" is
    /// about this address; "Film unavailable" is about the fetch and deliberately does not say the film
    /// does not exist, because a failed fetch knows nothing at all about that.
    /// </summary>
    internal const string NotFoundHeading = "Film not found";

    internal const string UnavailableHeading = "Film unavailable";

    /// <summary>
    /// The published key, from the route. A string, so a key the router cannot convert is junk to look up
    /// and not an unhandled exception -- there is nothing to parse here anyway.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Key { get; set; }

    private ElementReference heading;
    private ElementReference stateContainer;

    // Loading with a NULL value, which is the one place a null value is legitimate: CatalogService.GetFilmAsync
    // returns Ready with null for "the catalogue loaded and holds no film with that key", and this page's whole
    // not-found branch rests on telling that apart from Failed. An empty value still never means a failure.
    private CatalogResult<MovieRecord?> films = new(CatalogState.Loading, null);

    private CatalogMeta meta = CatalogMeta.Empty;

    /// <summary>The film, or null in every state that does not have one. Ready-with-null is not-found.</summary>
    private MovieRecord? film => films.State == CatalogState.Ready ? films.Value : null;

    // The key the current state was fetched for. The router REUSES this component when one film's address is
    // replaced by another's, so without this the page would show the first film for ever; comparing keys rather
    // than counting renders is what makes OnParametersSetAsync idempotent over the renders Blazor makes for its
    // own reasons.
    private string? loadedKey;

    private bool disposed;
    private bool retrying;

    // True once a render has committed while the fetch was still in flight, which is the whole condition on
    // saying the film's name aloud. See the Ready arm.
    private bool wasLoading;

    // True once THIS arrival has been reported, by whichever channel did it: the status announcement or the
    // focus move after a successful retry. Reset when the key changes, because a second film is a second
    // thing the reader needs telling about.
    private bool named;

    private bool failureReported;
    private bool movedFocusToFailure;
    private bool movingFocus;
    private bool focusHeadingAfterRetry;

    /// <summary>
    /// The heading, per state. It is also the page title and, in the two Ready states, the name the page
    /// announces -- one string for all three, so the tab, the outline and the announcement cannot disagree.
    /// </summary>
    private string Heading => films.State switch
    {
        CatalogState.Failed => UnavailableHeading,
        CatalogState.Loading => LoadingVisible,
        _ => films.Value?.T ?? NotFoundHeading,
    };

    /// <summary>
    /// The one sentence in the state container, for the three states with no film in them. In the fourth the
    /// container holds the film's byline instead and this is never read.
    /// </summary>
    private string Sentence => films.State switch
    {
        CatalogState.Failed => CouldNotLoad,
        CatalogState.Loading => LoadingVisible,
        _ => NoSuchFilm,
    };

    /// <summary>
    /// The genre names this film carries, comma-separated, from <c>meta.json</c>'s map. An id the meta does
    /// not name is DROPPED rather than rendered as a number: "28" tells a reader nothing, and a half-deployed
    /// meta is exactly when it would happen.
    /// </summary>
    private string GenreNames(MovieRecord f) => string.Join(", ", f.G
        .Select(id => meta.Genres.GetValueOrDefault(id.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)))
        .Where(name => name is { Length: > 0 }));

    /// <summary>
    /// What the match was, in the band's own words and with the score beside them. The words come from
    /// <c>ConfidenceBand</c> and are never retyped here, so the page, the tile and the build summary the
    /// owner reads cannot drift apart.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The Confident case says so in words rather than returning null as <c>BadgeFor</c> does. A badge on
    /// 1,265 of 1,456 tiles would train the eye to ignore the ones that matter, which is why the tile shows
    /// nothing; one row in a description list on one film's page has no such cost, and leaving it out would
    /// make "no Match row" mean both "matched well" and "a field this page forgot".
    /// <para>
    /// An unmatched record's score is 0 and means nothing -- the pipeline writes 0 whatever the best
    /// candidate scored -- so it is not shown. The percentage is formatted in the browser's culture, as
    /// every other number on this site is.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    private static string MatchSentence(MovieRecord f)
    {
        var band = ConfidenceBand.Of(f);

        if (band == Confidence.Unmatched) return ConfidenceBand.BadgeFor(band)!;

        var score = f.Mc.ToString("P0");

        return band == Confidence.Confident ? "Matched, " + score : ConfidenceBand.BadgeFor(band) + ", " + score;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The owner's own fields, in a stable order, with blank names and blank values dropped. See the markup
    /// for why the order is Ordinal and why a blank row is absent rather than padded.
    /// </summary>
    private static List<KeyValuePair<string, string>> ExtraFields(MovieRecord f) => f.Ex is null
        ? []
        : [.. f.Ex
            .Where(p => p.Key is { Length: > 0 } && p.Value is { Length: > 0 })
            .OrderBy(p => p.Key, StringComparer.Ordinal)];

    /// <remarks>
    /// EVERY arrival comes through here and not through <c>OnInitializedAsync</c>, because the router reuses
    /// this component when one film's address replaces another's: with the fetch in OnInitialized the second
    /// film would never be looked up. Guarded on the key so that the renders Blazor makes for its own reasons
    /// cost no work.
    /// <para>
    /// Nothing is announced here. Rule R4: a message raised before the first render starts its settle timer
    /// while the clearing render is still queued, so the real gap collapses on exactly the slow devices the
    /// gap exists for. Everything this page says it says from <see cref="OnAfterRenderAsync"/>. The clear
    /// below is a cancellation, which takes effect at once and speaks nothing.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    protected override async Task OnParametersSetAsync()
    {
        if (string.Equals(loadedKey, Key, StringComparison.Ordinal)) return;

        loadedKey = Key;
        films = new(CatalogState.Loading, null);
        wasLoading = false;
        named = false;
        failureReported = false;
        movedFocusToFailure = false;
        Announcer.Status("");

        var result = await FetchAsync();
        if (disposed) return;

        films = result;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The film and the genre names, in parallel; the film decides the outcome. A meta that failed is
    /// <c>CatalogMeta.Empty</c> -- no genre names, every other row intact -- so its state is deliberately
    /// not looked at: this page owns one failure sentence and a second voice for the same fetch would be two.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The fetch is awaited and never cancelled. The task is shared and cached by the service, so cancelling
    /// it would turn one navigation away into a Failed result for the next page that wanted the catalogue.
    /// </remarks>
    private async Task<CatalogResult<MovieRecord?>> FetchAsync()
    {
        var filmRequest = Catalog.GetFilmAsync(Key ?? string.Empty);
        var metaRequest = Catalog.GetMetaAsync();

        var result = await filmRequest;
        if (result.State == CatalogState.Ready) meta = (await metaRequest).Value;

        return result;
    }

    /// <remarks>
    /// Every announcement and every focus move, after the render that carries the new state.
    /// </remarks>
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (disposed) return;

        switch (films.State)
        {
            case CatalogState.Loading:
                // Nothing is said. The heading itself reads "Loading film", and FocusOnNavigate has just put
                // focus on it, so a screen reader has already spoken those words -- rule R3 forbids announcing
                // the text of whatever focus has just moved to. The flag is what the Ready arm needs.
                wasLoading = true;
                break;

            case CatalogState.Failed:
                // THE FOCUS MOVE IS THE PRIMARY CHANNEL and it runs FIRST, because a screen reader reads the
                // contents of a focused unnamed tabindex="-1" container and this container's whole text IS the
                // failure sentence. A move that happens therefore already says it, in the words on screen.
                // The flag is set only when the move actually happened, so a reader who had the nav drawer open
                // when the fetch failed is not denied the move for ever.
                var movedOnThisPass = false;
                if (!movedFocusToFailure && !movingFocus)
                {
                    movingFocus = true;
                    try { movedOnThisPass = movedFocusToFailure = await FocusIfUnmoved(stateContainer); }
                    finally { movingFocus = false; }
                }

                // R11: one report for one failure the reader can see, and THE ALERT IS THE FALLBACK -- raised
                // only where the focus move did not happen. Raising both speaks the sentence twice: once as the
                // contents of the container focus landed on, and again a settle delay later when the assertive
                // region interrupts with identical text. On a FAILED RETRY focus is usually already on the Try
                // again button, which is not a keeper, so the helper refuses, nothing moves, and this fires --
                // which is how a reader who retries and fails again is not met with silence. "Usually" is doing
                // work in that sentence; see the remarks on PageFocus.Keepers.
                // movingFocus is in the condition because a render can arrive while the first pass's focus call
                // is suspended at its await: that pass has not chosen a channel yet and this one must not
                // choose for it.
                if (!failureReported && !movingFocus)
                {
                    failureReported = true;
                    if (!movedOnThisPass) Announcer.Alert(CouldNotLoad);
                }

                break;

            case CatalogState.Ready:
                // A SUCCESSFUL RETRY, and the focus move is the channel. The Try again button the reader was
                // standing on has gone, so focus would otherwise drop to <body>; landing on the heading, which
                // now holds the film's name, says the name as it lands. Nothing is announced after it, for the
                // R3 reason the Loading arm gives.
                if (focusHeadingAfterRetry)
                {
                    focusHeadingAfterRetry = false;
                    named = true;
                    await FocusOutright(heading);
                }

                // THE NAME, ONCE, AND ONLY WHERE THE READER HAS NOT ALREADY HEARD IT. wasLoading is the whole
                // guard and it is not a micro-optimisation: when the catalogue is already in memory -- which is
                // every arrival from a tile on the films page, the common case -- this page is Ready on its
                // FIRST render, so FocusOnNavigate lands on a heading that already holds the film's name and a
                // reader hears it from the focus move. Announcing as well would be the same words twice for one
                // event. On a cold deep link the heading said "Loading film" when focus landed, nothing has
                // said the film's name since, and this is the only channel that will.
                // Status and not Alert: an arrival is not a failure, and the polite region never interrupts.
                if (!named && wasLoading)
                {
                    named = true;
                    Announcer.Status(Heading);
                }

                break;
        }
    }

    /// <remarks>
    /// Nothing auto-retries and nothing auto-dismisses. The failed text is a state, not a toast: a message
    /// that cleared itself would be a content-set time limit on reading it, SC 2.2.1 Timing Adjustable,
    /// Level A, and an auto-retry with a visible countdown would engage the same criterion.
    /// <para>
    /// There is no Try again in the not-found state, and that is the point of telling it from a failure: the
    /// address is wrong, so a retry is a button that cannot help.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    private async Task TryAgain()
    {
        // The button carries aria-disabled rather than the disabled attribute, so the click still arrives
        // while a retry is in flight and has to be ignored here.
        if (retrying) return;

        // R5: the failure has ended as far as the reader is concerned. A cancellation, not a message -- it
        // takes effect at once and speaks nothing. The next Alert is raised from OnAfterRenderAsync and never
        // in this handler: on a device slow enough to flush both mutations in one frame the second call would
        // see the region already empty, mark the clear observed, and the repeated identical sentence would
        // reach the DOM as no change at all.
        Announcer.Alert("");

        retrying = true;
        failureReported = false;
        movedFocusToFailure = false;
        films = new(CatalogState.Loading, null);   // the loading copy, visibly

        var result = await FetchAsync();
        if (disposed) return;

        retrying = false;
        films = result;

        // Only a film earns the focus move: it is the heading's new text that reports the success, and
        // "Film not found" is this address being wrong rather than the retry working.
        focusHeadingAfterRetry = result is { State: CatalogState.Ready, Value: not null };
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Moves focus to <paramref name="element"/> unless the reader has moved focus themselves, and says
    /// whether it happened.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The guard cannot be written in C#: there is no way to read <c>document.activeElement</c> from Blazor,
    /// and <c>ElementReference.FocusAsync()</c> focuses unconditionally. index.html defines the helper and
    /// says why the move is a conformance route rather than a workaround.
    /// </remarks>
    private Task<bool> FocusIfUnmoved(ElementReference element) =>
        TryFocus(() => Js.InvokeAsync<bool>("movieLibrary.focusIfUnmoved", element, PageFocus.Keepers).AsTask());

    /// <summary>Moves focus to <paramref name="element"/> whatever holds it now.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The successful retry only: the button the reader was standing on has gone, so focus would otherwise
    /// drop to <c>&lt;body&gt;</c>. Nothing to ask the helper here -- the control that had focus no longer
    /// exists.
    /// </remarks>
    private static Task<bool> FocusOutright(ElementReference element) =>
        TryFocus(async () => { await element.FocusAsync(); return true; });

    /// <summary>Runs one focus move and says whether it happened, swallowing the ways interop can fail.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <c>ElementReference.FocusAsync()</c> is interop exactly as the helper call is -- it invokes
    /// <c>Blazor._internal.domWrapper.focus</c> -- so an escape from either reveals <c>#blazor-error-ui</c>,
    /// which carries an assertive role of its own, on a page that is already reporting a failure through the
    /// announcer or has just recovered from one: two assertive sources for one event, which is the thing this
    /// project forbids outright.
    /// </remarks>
    private static async Task<bool> TryFocus(Func<Task<bool>> move)
    {
        try
        {
            return await move();
        }
        catch (Exception ex) when (ex is JSException or TaskCanceledException or ObjectDisposedException)
        {
            return false;
        }
    }

    /// <remarks>
    /// R7: clear what this page set, and raise nothing new. Both calls are cancellations -- they take effect
    /// at once and speak nothing. Without them a film's name sits in #status-announcer, readable in browse
    /// mode, long after the reader has left the film, because the announcer is a scoped singleton and
    /// MainLayout survives navigation.
    /// <para>
    /// #route-announcer is NOT cleared here, and that is deliberate rather than an omission. RouteAnnouncer
    /// is its only caller -- SourceContractTests pins that -- and it writes the region empty on the way in to
    /// this page and the next page's name on the way out. A clear from here would land after that write on
    /// any navigation where this component is disposed last, and the latest-wins rule would drop the name of
    /// the page the reader just arrived at.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    public void Dispose()
    {
        disposed = true;

        Announcer.Status("");
        Announcer.Alert("");
    }
}
